WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.146
<v Examiner>Near vision.

2
00:00:02.146 --> 00:00:02.882
<v Examiner>Three marks.

3
00:00:03.332 --> 00:00:06.456
<v Examiner>What changes occur in the eye to allow near vision?

4
00:00:06.456 --> 00:00:09.028
<v Examiner>And how is this undone for distant vision?

5
00:00:09.478 --> 00:00:11.067
Three marks, three changes.

6
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:14.363
Picture yourself reading a book held close to your face.

7
00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:16.667
The hook: bulge, shrink, cross.

8
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.434
The lens bulges, the pupils shrink, and the eyes cross inwards.

9
00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:23.005
Look out of the window, and all three undo.

10
00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:23.843
Bulge.

11
00:00:23.843 --> 00:00:27.978
The ciliary muscle contracts, the suspensory ligaments go slack,

12
00:00:27.978 --> 00:00:32.758
and the elastic lens recoils into a rounder shape, so it bends light more.

13
00:00:32.758 --> 00:00:34.890
That's accommodation of the lens.

14
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:35.781
Shrink.

15
00:00:35.781 --> 00:00:37.103
The pupils constrict.

16
00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.439
The circular, sphincter muscles of the iris contract.

17
00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:41.287
Cross.

18
00:00:41.287 --> 00:00:47.203
The eyes converge: both eyeballs rotate medially, turning inwards toward the near object.

19
00:00:48.653 --> 00:00:49.713
Why it's needed.

20
00:00:49.713 --> 00:00:53.090
Light from a near object arrives as diverging rays.

21
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.195
Light from more than six metres away arrives as parallel rays.

22
00:00:57.195 --> 00:01:00.771
The near rays need more bending to land on the retina.

23
00:01:01.221 --> 00:01:03.376
For distant vision, all three undo.

24
00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:07.380
The ciliary muscle relaxes, the lens flattens, the pupils dilate,

25
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.413
and the eyes look straight ahead.

26
00:01:10.863 --> 00:01:11.516
The traps.

27
00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:13.998
For near vision, the pupil constricts.

28
00:01:13.998 --> 00:01:16.871
Her student-marked feedback says it dilates,

29
00:01:16.871 --> 00:01:20.659
but her own quiz key and both of her decks say constricts.

30
00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:21.769
Write constricts.

31
00:01:21.769 --> 00:01:25.688
Second: the ciliary muscle contracts to make the lens bulge.

32
00:01:25.688 --> 00:01:29.672
Her true or false trap says it contracts to flatten the lens.

33
00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:30.521
That's false.

34
00:01:30.521 --> 00:01:33.786
Third: one of her slides calls the lens biconcave.

35
00:01:33.786 --> 00:01:34.701
It's biconvex.

36
00:01:35.151 --> 00:01:35.781
<v Examiner>Once more.

37
00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:36.537
<v Examiner>Near vision.

38
00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:44.299
The ciliary muscle contracts, so the lens bulges and gets rounder: accommodation.

39
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:45.676
The pupils constrict.

40
00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:47.971
The eyes converge, turning inwards.

41
00:01:47.971 --> 00:01:53.349
For distant vision, all three undo: the ciliary muscle relaxes, the lens flattens,

42
00:01:53.349 --> 00:01:54.792
and the pupils dilate.

43
00:01:54.792 --> 00:01:59.382
Near light arrives diverging; from over six metres away it's parallel.
